1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,320
Tuesday night,
the fifth of December, 2017.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:08,160
Meet Elsie. She's fifty-eight,
she lives in Paarl,

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,400
and she has saved R100,000
over thirty years.

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,640
All of it sits in one company.

5
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,600
The company that owns the
Pep store down her road.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,800
Steinhoff. That night,
one share costs about R46.

7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,600
Two days later, it costs R10.

8
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:31,280
Elsie's R100,000 is now
worth about R21,900.

9
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,440
She didn't spend a cent.

10
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,160
She didn't do anything wrong.

11
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,360
Remember that number, R21,900.

12
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:40,720
We'll come back to it.

13
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:42,560
And it wasn't just Elsie.

14
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,560
The pension fund for South Africa's
government workers owned nine percent of

15
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,720
Steinhoff. Teachers,
nurses, police officers.

16
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,880
On paper, their fund
lost about R20 billion.

17
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:00,720
So how does a company worth almost
R200 billion collapse in two days?

18
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:05,200
And how do you make sure one company
can never do this to your money?

19
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:10,640
Here's the answer up front: never let
one company hold all your savings.

20
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:14,960
Spread it out. By the end of this
video, you'll see exactly why,

21
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:18,240
and the warning signs
that were there for years.

22
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:20,000
Let's start from zero.

23
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,120
Steinhoff started as a
small furniture business.

24
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,160
By 2016, it was run from Stellenbosch,
and it was everywhere.

25
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:32,320
It bought Pep and Ackermans'
owner, Pepkor, in 2015.

26
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:34,240
It bought Poundland in Britain.

27
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:39,200
It bought Mattress Firm in America
for about 3.8 billion dollars.

28
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:44,000
By 2016, it had six and a half
thousand stores, forty brands,

29
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,600
in thirty countries.
Big pension funds bought it.

30
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,200
Unit trusts bought it.

31
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:53,440
Ordinary people like Elsie bought
it, because everyone else had.

32
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,720
To an investor like Elsie,
it looked perfect.

33
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,360
Profits going up. A famous boss.

34
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,720
Shops she could actually walk into.

35
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:06,800
What could go wrong? But here's the
question nobody was asking loudly enough.

36
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:10,000
Where was all that profit
really coming from?

37
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:12,080
Imagine you run a spaza shop.

38
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:17,440
Business is slow. So you "sell" a thousand
rand of stock to your cousin's shop next

39
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:20,000
door. You write it down as profit.

40
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:21,840
Your books look great.

41
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:26,133
Next year, to hide the hole,
you do it again, only bigger.

42
00:02:26,187 --> 00:02:28,293
But your cousin never pays.

43
00:02:28,347 --> 00:02:29,720
And your cousin's shop?

44
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:31,960
You secretly control it.

45
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,920
That, in very simple terms, is what
investigators found at Steinhoff.

46
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:41,880
In 2019, the forensic firm P
W C published its findings.

47
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,227
Over nine years,

48
00:02:43,253 --> 00:02:48,773
there were fictitious or irregular
deals worth about 6.5 billion euros.

49
00:02:48,827 --> 00:02:51,440
That's more than R100 billion.

50
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:56,000
The deals were with companies that
were "made to appear" independent.

51
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:59,280
But they were linked to the
same small group of people.

52
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:03,480
Some documents were created
after the fact, and backdated.

53
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,440
So the profits looked real.

54
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,960
The shops were real. The money?

55
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:13,280
A lot of it wasn't. Now, you might think
a trick that big would be invisible.

56
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,280
It wasn't. The warning
signs were there for years.

57
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:22,240
And one of them, you can check yourself,
in five minutes, for any company.

58
00:03:23,118 --> 00:03:26,718
Warning sign one:
the numbers were hard to follow.

59
00:03:27,118 --> 00:03:29,438
As far back as 2007,

60
00:03:29,598 --> 00:03:34,478
analysts asked why the accounts didn't
clearly show where the revenue came from.

61
00:03:34,598 --> 00:03:36,278
Steinhoff pushed back.

62
00:03:36,558 --> 00:03:40,238
Warning sign two: deals with insiders.

63
00:03:40,318 --> 00:03:44,958
Money moved between Steinhoff and
companies linked to its own people.

64
00:03:45,038 --> 00:03:47,838
Warning sign three: the police.

65
00:03:47,878 --> 00:03:49,598
In November 2015,

66
00:03:49,678 --> 00:03:54,558
German investigators raided Steinhoff's
European office over suspected accounting

67
00:03:54,718 --> 00:03:57,198
fraud. In August 2017,

68
00:03:57,278 --> 00:04:02,078
a German magazine reported that the
boss himself was under investigation.

69
00:04:02,118 --> 00:04:05,158
The share price dipped, then carried on.

70
00:04:05,438 --> 00:04:09,118
And warning sign four,
the one you can check yourself:

71
00:04:09,358 --> 00:04:11,998
growth that's too fast to explain.

72
00:04:12,118 --> 00:04:15,278
Steinhoff grew by buying
company after company,

73
00:04:15,358 --> 00:04:17,918
faster than anyone could check the books.

74
00:04:17,998 --> 00:04:22,158
If you can't explain how a company
makes its money in one sentence,

75
00:04:22,238 --> 00:04:24,438
that's your sign to slow down.

76
00:04:24,718 --> 00:04:26,958
Elsie didn't see any of this.

77
00:04:27,038 --> 00:04:30,478
Most people didn't.
The share price was still high,

78
00:04:30,518 --> 00:04:33,438
and a high price feels like proof.

79
00:04:33,598 --> 00:04:36,238
Then came December 2017.

80
00:04:36,318 --> 00:04:40,078
On the evening of the fifth of
December, the auditors, Deloitte,

81
00:04:40,158 --> 00:04:42,185
wouldn't sign off on the results.

82
00:04:42,211 --> 00:04:44,105
The results were postponed.

83
00:04:44,131 --> 00:04:47,518
And the CEO, Markus Jooste, resigned.

84
00:04:47,758 --> 00:04:53,318
Wednesday morning, the share fell
sixty-one percent, to R17.61.

85
00:04:53,358 --> 00:04:57,398
Thursday, another
forty-three percent, to R10.

86
00:04:57,438 --> 00:05:00,598
About eighty percent gone in two days.

87
00:05:00,638 --> 00:05:04,438
Roughly ten billion euros
of value, wiped out.

88
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:07,518
Well over R100 billion.

89
00:05:07,598 --> 00:05:10,438
On Thursday, Elsie checks her statement.

90
00:05:10,478 --> 00:05:15,251
R21,900. Thirty years of saving,

91
00:05:15,305 --> 00:05:18,838
and nearly eighty
percent gone in two days.

92
00:05:18,878 --> 00:05:22,691
Who lost? Big funds, and ordinary people.

93
00:05:22,745 --> 00:05:26,358
The government workers'
fund, about R20 billion.

94
00:05:26,398 --> 00:05:30,598
Its pensions weren't cut, because
that fund promises a set pension,

95
00:05:30,638 --> 00:05:32,518
but the money was gone.

96
00:05:32,558 --> 00:05:34,905
Years later, in 2022,

97
00:05:34,931 --> 00:05:39,225
a court approved a settlement
of 1.43 billion euros,

98
00:05:39,331 --> 00:05:44,398
about R25 billion. More
than 43,000 claims came in,

99
00:05:44,478 --> 00:05:47,158
worth about 3.2 billion euros.

100
00:05:47,198 --> 00:05:51,251
So most people got back only a
small part of what they lost.

101
00:05:51,305 --> 00:05:53,331
And in October 2023,

102
00:05:53,385 --> 00:05:57,878
the shares of Steinhoff International
officially ceased to exist.

103
00:05:57,918 --> 00:06:00,038
What happened to the people at the top?

104
00:06:00,078 --> 00:06:04,198
Jooste was banned from running a
listed company for twenty years.

105
00:06:04,238 --> 00:06:05,958
In March 2024,

106
00:06:05,998 --> 00:06:10,931
regulators fined him
a record R475 million.

107
00:06:10,985 --> 00:06:15,038
He died the next day, before
he ever faced a criminal court.

108
00:06:15,118 --> 00:06:18,638
Since then, several former
executives have been convicted.

109
00:06:18,798 --> 00:06:22,878
The former finance boss got an
effective five years in prison.

110
00:06:23,358 --> 00:06:24,758
And here's the twist.

111
00:06:24,958 --> 00:06:28,158
The shops were fine. Pep is still open.

112
00:06:28,318 --> 00:06:32,878
Pepkor is still listed, with more
than six and a half thousand stores.

113
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:38,078
In July 2025, what's left of
Steinhoff, now called Ibex,

114
00:06:38,158 --> 00:06:43,718
sold its whole Pepkor stake for
about R27 billion, to pay its debts.

115
00:06:43,918 --> 00:06:47,118
The shops were real. The accounts weren't.

116
00:06:47,278 --> 00:06:52,398
And the people who paid were the ones
who trusted one company with everything.

117
00:06:53,358 --> 00:06:55,558
So what does this mean for you?

118
00:06:55,838 --> 00:06:57,438
Let's go back to Elsie.

119
00:06:57,518 --> 00:07:00,798
She put R100,000 into one company.

120
00:07:00,878 --> 00:07:05,118
Two days later, she had about R21,900.

121
00:07:05,198 --> 00:07:08,478
Now imagine she split it
across twenty companies.

122
00:07:08,558 --> 00:07:12,078
R5,000 each. One of them is Steinhoff.

123
00:07:12,158 --> 00:07:18,158
Same crash. Her Steinhoff
R5,000 drops to about R1,100.

124
00:07:18,238 --> 00:07:20,238
The other nineteen don't care.

125
00:07:20,318 --> 00:07:23,758
She still has about R96,100.

126
00:07:23,878 --> 00:07:28,478
Even if Steinhoff went to
zero, she'd have R95,000.

127
00:07:28,598 --> 00:07:30,798
Same company. Same fraud.

128
00:07:30,838 --> 00:07:34,158
A loss of four percent,
instead of seventy-eight.

129
00:07:34,238 --> 00:07:36,078
That's diversification.

130
00:07:36,158 --> 00:07:37,758
It doesn't stop a fraud.

131
00:07:37,838 --> 00:07:41,038
It just makes sure one
fraud can't wipe you out.

132
00:07:41,118 --> 00:07:43,678
And the law already does
it for your pension.

133
00:07:43,758 --> 00:07:45,358
Under Regulation 28,

134
00:07:45,385 --> 00:07:49,918
a retirement fund can't put more than
fifteen percent of its money into even the

135
00:07:49,998 --> 00:07:52,051
biggest single listed company.

136
00:07:52,105 --> 00:07:55,158
You can do it too,
with a simple index fund.

137
00:07:55,198 --> 00:07:59,438
One index fund can hold the forty
biggest companies on the JSE.

138
00:07:59,558 --> 00:08:03,438
Steinhoff was less than two percent
of a big South African index,

139
00:08:03,518 --> 00:08:08,051
so when it crashed, that index lost
only about one point four percent.

140
00:08:08,105 --> 00:08:13,718
A tax-free savings account lets you invest
up to R46,000 a year in funds like that,

141
00:08:13,758 --> 00:08:15,678
with no tax on the growth.

142
00:08:15,758 --> 00:08:19,971
Our TFSA guide shows you how
to open one, step by step.

143
00:08:20,025 --> 00:08:24,691
And before you ever buy one company's
shares, run the four checks.

144
00:08:24,745 --> 00:08:27,891
Can I explain how it makes
money in one sentence?

145
00:08:27,945 --> 00:08:30,025
Are there deals with insiders?

146
00:08:30,051 --> 00:08:32,851
Is it growing faster
than anyone can check?

147
00:08:32,905 --> 00:08:36,238
Are the results late,
or is the auditor unhappy?

148
00:08:36,318 --> 00:08:39,478
One company can fail, even a famous one.

149
00:08:39,518 --> 00:08:44,158
Twenty companies, or forty,
almost never all fail together.

150
00:08:44,238 --> 00:08:49,278
So, the lesson from Steinhoff:
the shops were real, the profits weren't,

151
00:08:49,358 --> 00:08:51,878
and one company held everything.

152
00:08:51,918 --> 00:08:55,078
Spread your money, and it
can't take you down with it.

153
00:08:55,118 --> 00:08:57,651
Next, watch our tax-free savings guide,

154
00:08:57,705 --> 00:09:00,598
and see how to start with
just a few hundred rand.

155
